  "id": "Compound::DB00338 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Omeprazole is linked to Cyp2c9 through a series of molecular interactions that involve cytochrome P450 enzymes. The evidence suggests that Omeprazole binds to Cyp2c18 and Cyp2c8, which are both part of the Cyp2c family. These enzymes are known to participate in oxidative metabolism, and Cyp2c9 is a well-characterized member of this family that is involved in the metabolism of various substrates. The presence of Omeprazole in the context of Cyp2c19 inhibitors, which include Efavirenz, further supports a potential role for Omeprazole in modulating Cyp2c9 activity. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Omeprazole influences Cyp2c9, likely through its ability to inhibit or modulate the activity of Cyp2c family members. This relationship underscores the importance of Cyp2c9 in drug metabolism and suggests that Omeprazole may exert its effects through interactions with this enzyme complex.",
